.TH CBMAP_INIT_CC 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_init_cc \- create an ordered map with a custom comparison procedure into an already declared handle
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_init_cc(hm_name, custom_comparison_proc);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates an ordered map whose keys are ordered by custom_comparison_proc
instead of the default order for the declared key type, and assigns it to
hm_name. The caller must declare the handle first, with cbmap_declare(3)
or cbmap_declare_scoped(3). The key type and the value type are not
arguments; the macro reads them from the companion type variables that
the declare macro created.

This is cbmap_init(3) with a comparison procedure, and the allocator is
the default one. cbmap_init_mp(3) replaces the allocator instead, and
cbmap_init_full(3) takes both.

A struct key type needs this variant. Without a procedure the map orders
a key type it doesn't recognize by a raw comparison of the key's
representation, which reads the padding bytes and orders a pointer member
by its address.

The one-step equivalents are cbmap_construct_cc(3) and
cbmap_construct_cc_scoped(3).
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name                 The map handle that the caller declared earlier,
                        which receives the new map
custom_comparison_proc  A ccol_comparison_proc_t, or NULL for the
                        default order for the declared key type
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro expands to a statement, not to an expression.
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM COMPARISON"
.nf
The procedure receives pointers to two keys. It returns a negative value
when the first key orders before the second, a positive value when the
first orders after the second, and zero when the map must treat them as
the same key. A zero makes the call an update instead of an insert, which
is why the procedure defines key equality as well as key order.

The order must be a strict weak order over the key type that doesn't
change while the map holds elements. A given pair of keys always compares
the same way, the reverse comparison gives the opposite sign, and the
order is transitive. If the procedure is inconsistent the placement
invariant of the tree breaks, and the map can't find keys that it
holds.

The procedure gets two bare pointers and no lengths, so it can only read a
width that the declared key type fixes. For that reason the map checks the
width of an incoming key against that type and aligns its stored keys for
it; the procedure decides the order of the keys and nothing else.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through _ccol_fatal_err() if the creation
fails, and it uses the default allocator. The procedure must stay callable
for the whole life of the map.

A walk follows this procedure: it visits the elements in ascending key
order, by the order that the map was given, as cbmap_begin_iter(3)
describes.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
typedef struct { int major; int minor; } version;

int version_cmp(const void *a, const void *b) {
    version x, y;
    memcpy(&x, a, sizeof(x));   /* a key can be at any address */
    memcpy(&y, b, sizeof(y));
    if (x.major != y.major) {
        return (x.major > y.major) - (x.major < y.major);
    }
    return (x.minor > y.minor) - (x.minor < y.minor);
}

cbmap_declare(releases, version, char *);
cbmap_init_cc(releases, version_cmp);
cbmap_destroy(releases);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
